-- title:
--   Zariski sheaf property for Deligne data over a ring
-- statement:
--   Fix a commutative ring $\mathcal O$, a field $K$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, an element $\pi \in \mathcal O$, and a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$. Let $n \in \mathbb N$ and $f : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to B$ be such that the ideal generated by the $f_i$ is all of $B$; for each $i$ let $L_i$ be a commutative ring which is simultaneously a $B$-algebra and an $\mathcal O$-algebra compatibly, and which is a localisation of $B$ away from $f_i$; for each pair $(i,j)$ let $L_{ij}$ likewise be a localisation of $B$ away from $f_i f_j$ over $\mathcal O$ and $B$. Let $l_{ij} : L_i \to L_{ij}$ and $r_{ij} : L_j \to L_{ij}$ be $\mathcal O$-algebra maps whose composites with the structure maps from $B$ are the structure map $B \to L_{ij}$. Here an element of `OmegaObj` over a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $C$ is a Deligne datum: an assignment, to every full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M \subset K^2$, of a $C$-submodule of $C \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, compatible with inclusions of lattices, equivariant for scalar homotheties by $K^\times$, and satisfying, at every prime $\mathfrak p$ of $C$, the stated non-degeneracy condition involving multiplication by $\pi$ on a pair of nested lattices; `DeligneDatum.map` transports such a datum along an $\mathcal O$-algebra map by spanning with the image of each submodule. The conclusion is the conjunction: (i) two Deligne data over $B$ whose transports to every $L_i$ agree are equal; (ii) every family $x_i$ of Deligne data over the $L_i$ whose transports along $l_{ij}$ and $r_{ij}$ agree for all $i,j$ arises as the family of transports of a single Deligne datum over $B$.
--
--   This is the sheaf condition for Drinfeld's formal upper half plane functor $\widehat\Omega$, in the Deligne-datum model, with respect to a finite cover of $\operatorname{Spec} B$ by basic open sets: the diagram $\widehat\Omega(B) \to \prod_i \widehat\Omega(L_i) \rightrightarrows \prod_{i,j} \widehat\Omega(L_{ij})$ is an equaliser, with no Noetherian or $\pi$-nilpotency hypothesis. It is used to record that $\widehat\Omega$ is a Zariski sheaf and, with $\mathcal O = \mathbb Z_p$ and $\pi = p$, in the construction and representability statements for Drinfeld's period map.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalOmega_DeligneDatum_eq_of_forall_map_eq_and_exists_forall_map_eq_of_span_eq_top.lean

